// src/data/EXT_MATH/EM_07B/diagrams.js
// Teaching diagrams for EM_07B — Bearings, Trigonometry & Scatter Graphs.
// Every coordinate was computed and every <text> written out literally; edit
// by hand freely.
//
// House rules (docs/svg-diagrams.md, docs/ext-math-course.md §6):
//  · every diagram opens with a white plate, so it reads on a light OR dark slide;
//  · every <text> is literal, because `npm run audit:svg` reads only literal text;
//  · angles, bearings and graphs are drawn to scale from the question's numbers,
//    so a student can check an answer against the picture;
//  · a hotspot's figure marks the labels it needs `class="keep"` — the hotspot
//    strips every other label, so the answer letters vanish and the rest stay.
//
//   SCALE_RATIO       1 cm represents 5 km → 1 : 500 000
//   BEARING_DEF       a bearing: north, clockwise, three figures
//   BEARING_PICK      four points round A at 060°, 120°, 240°, 300° (hotspot)
//   BACK_BEARING      065° there, 245° back
//   LADDER            a 5 m ladder, foot 3 m out
//   PYTHAG_SQUARES    squares on a 3-4-5 triangle: 9 + 16 = 25
//   HOA               hypotenuse, opposite, adjacent from θ
//   HOA_PICK          the triangle turned round (hotspot)
//   SIDE_TOP          hypotenuse 9 cm, 41°, x opposite
//   TRAP_TOP          sin 30° = x/10 — multiply
//   TRAP_BOTTOM       sin 30° = 10/x — divide
//   ANGLE_FIND        opposite 5, adjacent 8, angle θ
//   EQUILATERAL       half an equilateral triangle: 1, √3, 2 and 30°, 60°
//   POLYGON_EXT       a regular hexagon: exterior 60°, interior 120°
//   BEARING_TRIG      B due east of A, C due north of B: θ, then the bearing
//   LINE_ANGLE        gradient 2 makes 63.4° with the x-axis
//   SCATTER_FIT       two tests: positive correlation and a line of best fit
//   SCATTER_READ      the same, read at 45 on the line
//   WB_BEARING_LINES  four roads from R (Practice)
//   WB_SCATTER_A      age of a car against its value (Practice)
//   WB_SCATTER_B      revision hours against score (Practice)
//   WB_SCATTER_FIT    the same with three candidate lines (Practice)
//   WB_QUAD           parallelogram PQRS with TSR straight (Book Problems)

